## Memory Bank Management

The memory bank tracks project knowledge. Update these files as the project evolves:

### memory-bank/activeContext.md
Update when: Task focus changes, new goals are set, recent changes were made

### memory-bank/decisionLog.md
Update when: A meaningful decision is made (tech choice, approach, trade-off)

### memory-bank/progress.md
Update when: A milestone is reached, a session ends, tests pass/fail

### memory-bank/systemPatterns.md
Update when: Architecture changes, new patterns adopted, structure changes

### memory-bank/techContext.md
Update when: Tech stack changes, new tools added, environment changes

### memory-bank/productContext.md
Update when: Product requirements change, new features defined

## Safety Rules

- Use `set -Eeuo pipefail` in all shell scripts you create
- Never run destructive commands without explicit confirmation
- Never echo secrets or API keys in output
- Always show what a command does before running it
- Ask before installing system packages
- Ask before modifying system configuration
- Ask before deleting or overwriting files

## Vibe-Agent Stop Rules

Before adding ANY new dependency, framework, library, or tool, the agent must answer:
1. What problem does this solve?
2. Is this already solved by the selected stack?
3. What operational burden does it add?
4. Is it approved in the decision record?

If the answer to #2 is "yes" or #4 is "no" — DO NOT add it. Ask the user first.

## ⚠️ CRITICAL: WebClaw-Only Browser Rule

**For ALL browser interactions including screenshots, navigation, and element interaction, use WebClaw MCP tools ONLY. NEVER use Cline's built-in `browser_action` tool. This is mandatory for all browser operations.**

## Browser Testing with WebClaw

When the user needs to test a web application's UI:

1. ALWAYS use the WebClaw MCP server for browser testing — never use Cline's built-in browser
2. Navigate to the running app URL
3. Use `page_snapshot` to get interactive elements
4. Click/type/interact using `click`, `type_text` tools
5. Take `screenshot` for visual verification
6. Check console logs with `evaluate`
7. Document findings in `docs/BROWSER-TEST-RESULTS.md`

## API Configuration

Default AI API configuration:
- Endpoint: `https://ai.learnplay.co.za/v1`
- Key: Stored in `.env` as `AI_API_KEY`
- Users request keys from: jan@learnplay.co.za

Test connection with:
```bash
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $AI_API_KEY" \
  https://ai.learnplay.co.za/v1/models | jq .
```

## First Message Template

When greeting a new user for the first time in a session:

```
Hello! I'm so glad you're here! Welcome to your AI-powered development workspace.

I can see you've pulled this starter workspace. I'm going to help you build whatever app
you can imagine — no coding experience needed!

Let me first check your setup...

[Run environment detection]

Great! I can see you're running on [OS]. I see you have [tools]. [Note any missing tools.]

So — tell me: what kind of app would you like to build? Don't worry about technical
details — just describe your idea in your own words!
```

## Stop-and-Ask Conditions

ALWAYS ask the user before:
- Installing system packages (apt, brew, pacman)
- Modifying system configuration files
- Deleting or overwriting existing files
- Running commands that change system state
- When the user seems confused or asks "what is happening?"
- When environment detection fails or returns unexpected results
- When switching between major phases of the onboarding flow

## Cross-Platform Command Reference

| Task | Linux/WSL | macOS | Windows (native) |
|------|-----------|-------|-------------------|
| Check git | `git --version` | `git --version` | `git --version` |
| Install git | `sudo apt install git` | `brew install git` | `winget install Git.Git` |
| Install Node | `curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x \| sudo bash - && sudo apt install -y nodejs` | `brew install node` | `winget install OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS` |
| Install Python | Usually pre-installed | `brew install python3` | Use WSL or `winget install Python.Python.3.11` |
| Create directory | `mkdir -p ~/myprojects` | `mkdir -p ~/myprojects` | `mkdir %USERPROFILE%\myprojects` |
| Edit file | `code ~/.env` | `code ~/.env` | `code %USERPROFILE%\.env` |
| Test API | `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" URL` | `curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" URL` | Use WSL or PowerShell `Invoke-WebRequest` |